The reason that the EVP_DigestSignInit() operation fails is that the supplied EC key only has a private key but not the public key.

This is actually a genuine problem with the construction of the EC_KEY, IMO (if you have a private key you should always have a public key). But it seems that for an EVP_DigestSigninit() operation in 1.1.1 we were tolerant of a missing public key (probably accidentally tolerant). Whether we are similarly tolerant for non-ec key types is unclear (I suspect not).

In 3.0, we check for this problem when you try and import the key into the provider - and it fails if there is no public portion. This was actually the case even in alpha5. What changed between alpha5 and alpha6 was that if we failed in the provider, then we fell back to legacy codepaths. We should not have been doing that - so the change between alpha5 and alpha6 is entirely correct, i.e. in effect a different bug in alpha5 masked this issue.

What we now have to decide is what do we do in response. Options are:

1) Relax the checks during import into the provider to allow this.

I am concerned that this could end up being the wrong path. There may well be other things that might break if you attempt to import such a key - so it is a good thing to test for this.

2) Infer the public key if there is no private key during the import (if we go down this route do we have to do it for all key types??)

3) Just declare that you're not allowed to do that. This seems like it could break some code.

@slontis - your thoughts?

