@levitte I think we have derailed the discussion: loading a key file that has no public key component is not a problem and is not under discussion!

In 1.1.1 (and in 3.0 as well, otherwise the `evp_tests` written by @bbbrumley would be failing) we have code that on decoding of private keys checks if the pubkey is missing and computes it at load time to uphold the documented assumption being discussed in the [vote proposal] (i.e., that a valid `EVP_PKEY` containing key material is either a public key or key pair; let's dub it "keypair assumption" for brevity).

https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/e2e09d9fba1187f8d6aafaa34d4172f56f1ffb72/crypto/ec/ec_asn1.c#L1020-L1036

The "keypair assumption", as noted by @bbbrumley, is not an unicum in OpenSSL, but is valid for many other crypto libraries (even not derived from OpenSSL).
Also, within OpenSSL, it's not something that happens only for certain encoding formats: most of our tests happen to test the `PEM` parsing codepaths, but in also the others that we tested in [Certified Side Channels] exhibit the same behavior, even when the private key format explicitly forbids to include also the pub key components, e.g. `PVK`, to enforce that parsing abides to the "keypair assumption".

What this issue raises as a "regression" (and I disagree) is that in 1.1.1, a user could programmatically create an `EC_KEY` object intentionally avoiding to set the public key component, then call `EVP_PKEY_assign()` violating the "keypair assumption" and execute certain operations that do not require the public key component without any failure happening: @mattcaswell has a very good writeup with actual code in https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/12612#issuecomment-694288286

If say, instead of `DigestSign` the operation to be executed with the "_incomplete `EVP_PKEY`_" was `DigestVerify` (or another the requires the public key component), the lack of enforcement **could** result in a segmentation fault (because in some parts of the code and in certain, even recent, periods of time we avoided `NULL` checks by design relying on the "keypair assumption"), and I believe until the issue of _the "keypair assumption" is probably stale documentation_ was raised, we would have ascribed such a segmentation fault to programmer error…

The lack of enforcement in my view was intentional (and went hand in hand with the enforcement of avoiding `NULL` checks seen as superfluous): we do not want to burden the execution of well behaving code that abides by the documented assumptions ("keypair assumption", do not pass `NULL` pointers unless explicitly allowed by the documentation, don't do silly things like casting a `(int *)` to `(EVP_PKEY *)` to feed garbage, etc.) with unnecessary checks, and we ascribe to "programmer error" not abiding to such assumptions.

### The case of an "incomplete" `EC_KEY` fed to `EVP_DigestVerify()`

In the previous paragraph I intentionally highlighted "could", because in the specific case of `EC_KEY` and `EVP_DigestVerify` I did experiment to see if we would hit a segfault or not, and indeed this is one instance in which we are including the required `NULL` checks (down the rabbit hole, once we reach `ossl_ecdsa_verify_sig()`):

https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/e2e09d9fba1187f8d6aafaa34d4172f56f1ffb72/crypto/ec/ecdsa_ossl.c#L317-L333

